# Faithfall — Game Specification v1.1

Nov 18, 2026 · @Alfred Reijo Philomin

## 1. Overview

**Faithfall: every Redditor is an Ascendant.** Each day, history leaps forward by years or centuries. You guide your people, leave relics behind, and when your civilization falls, you hope someone digs you back up.

It is a persistent, shared-world daily game for Reddit's Game of the Day 2026 hackathon. It runs on Devvit Web and the deadline is **Dec 8, 2026, 6:00pm PST (Dec 9, 7:30am IST)**. A daily session takes about 2 minutes.

### Design pillars

1. **Ascendants are immortal; civilizations are not.** No player is ever eliminated.
2. **Ascendants influence, never control.** Actions start processes and never guarantee results.
3. **Belief earns Trust.** Trust is the power you spend; it comes from how many followers you have and how strongly they believe.
4. **Time leaps unpredictably but for everyone at once.** One leap a day, the same for the whole world.
5. **The world remembers.** Artifacts, ruins and bloodlines outlive their Ascendants.
6. **The world is alive without you.** Old Ones (bot Ascendants), wild peoples and the seasonal Corruption keep history moving.

### How it scores on each judging criterion

| Criterion (25% each) | What earns it |
| --- | --- |
| Delightful UX | A 90-second Cradle tutorial that pays off instantly, one map screen, cards instead of menus, a map that ages visibly |
| Polish | Small, finished scope, one consistent art style, mobile-first, no dead buttons |
| Reddit-y | A daily Chronicle post, comments that work as diplomacy, upvoted scripture, a shared fight against the Evil One |
| Daily Content | A new leap and Chronicle every day, ruins to dig, a prophecy to make, Corruption seasons |

## 2. Core daily loop

One session is five steps and about 2 minutes. On a player's very first day, the Cradle tutorial (section 3) comes first. Every step either finishes something or gives the player a reason to come back tomorrow.

1. **Read the Chronicle** (20s). See how far history leapt ("80 years passed"), what happened to your people, and what happened in the world.
2. **Play cards** (40s). Draw 3 divine action cards and play up to 2 using Trust. Their results show up in later leaps.
3. **Dig ruins** (30s). Use 3 digs a day on ruin tiles to uncover fragments, artifacts or relics of forgotten Ascendants.
4. **Make a prophecy** (10s). Guess tomorrow's leap size or whether a rival will survive. A correct guess pays Trust.
5. **Share your myth** (20s). The end card shows a one-line myth about your Ascendant plus the year, with "Return tomorrow to see what history remembers." One tap posts it as a comment.

**Once a day, every day:** at **14:00 UTC (7:30pm IST / 6:00am PST)** the server queues all actions, resolves one leap for the whole world, and publishes a new Chronicle post.

**Returning players** get extra things to do on some days: give orders to a champion, help fight the Corruption, or write scripture. None of these ever stretch a session past about 3 minutes.

## 3. Ascendants

Each Redditor gets one Ascendant per world. Becoming an Ascendant takes about 40 seconds, most of it spent watching the Starry Draw.

### Onboarding

1. **The Starry Draw.** Seven sealed species cards flip over one by one. Each card rolls a rarity (section 4) and then picks a species that no player in this world has yet, or the least-taken one once all have been used. **The sign-up draw always succeeds:** all 7 cards reveal a species, and none can come up Silent. Failed draws exist only in the daily Seek the Lost. All 7 flip in about 3 seconds, and a tap skips the animation. You get one free redraw of the whole hand.
2. **Choose your people.** Each card shows the species' lifespan, speciality and starting conditions. A rarer card means a harder start.
3. **Pick a domain** from 5 illustrated cards.
4. **Accept or reroll a generated name** ("Vaelor, Keeper of the Drowned Bell").
5. **Enter the Cradle,** your private starting land, and play the tutorial (below).

### The Cradle (private tutorial world)

Every new Ascendant starts in **the Cradle**: a private pocket of 7 regions that only they can see, shaped to their species' home terrain (a sky-island for Duskwings, a sunken grotto for Tidefolk, a mountain hollow for Stonebound). It runs on the same simulation engine as the shared world, but **leaps happen instantly when you act**, not once a day. Those leaps never touch the shared world's clock.

The tutorial takes about 90 seconds. It can't be failed, and each beat teaches one thing and pays off on the spot:

1. **Bless:** play Bless Harvest. A 5-year leap happens straight away; Food rises and the village grows.
2. **Dig:** dig the ruin beside you. It always holds a relic of a fallen Old One, with its inscription, so the game's best moment happens in the first minute.
3. **Whisper:** Whisper to the wild village next door. A 25-year leap shows them converting, and your land grows.
4. **Omen:** a small patch of Corruption creeps in. Play Ward and watch the spread stop.
5. **Ascend:** your people leave the Cradle for the shared world.

**Landing:** some regions of the shared world are **reserved landing regions**, locked for incoming Ascendants. When you Ascend you choose one of 2–3 open landing regions that suit your species. Common and Uncommon landings sit away from strong empires; Rare and Epic landings are harsher (section 4). For your first 3 days, no one (player or Old One) can Smite or raid your lands.

**What comes with you:** your species, your people, the relic you dug up and your first scripture line. Your Cradle doesn't vanish. It becomes your **first ruin** in the shared world (section 7), waiting for someone to discover it.

The Cradle is played once. Returning players go straight to the shared world.

### Seek the Lost (a daily draw)

Once a day, an Ascendant can spend **15 base Trust** to call out to a lost people. This is how you get access to more species after joining.

| Result | Chance |
| --- | --- |
| No one answered (failed) | 50% |
| A Common people | 25% |
| An Uncommon people | 14% |
| A Rare people | 8% |
| An Epic people | 3% |

A people that answers appears as a wild region near your borders, or on the edge of the Dark Continent. You still have to Whisper to them to convert them, and they keep their own species. After 5 failures in a row, the next Seek is guaranteed to find an Uncommon people or rarer. **Seeking is paid with Trust only, never real money.**

### Domains

A domain decides which cards you draw more often and gives one passive bonus. No domain is stronger overall.

| Domain | Draws more of | Passive |
| --- | --- | --- |
| War | Smite, Teach Might | +10% Might growth |
| Harvest | Bless Harvest, Reveal Spring | +10% Food |
| Death | Whisper, Cleanse | Digs near ruins find relics more often |
| Sea | Inspire, Whisper | Can expand across water regions |
| Wisdom | Teach, Ward | Faster payoff on Teach cards |

### Ascendant attributes

| Attribute | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Name, epithet, domain, sigil | Identity, shown on the map and the share card |
| Trust | Spendable power. Start at 50, no cap |
| Remembrance | Points earned when people find your artifacts. Powers your return from fading |
| Scripture | One holy line you write, up to 80 characters |
| Status | Active, Fading or Returned |
| Streak | Days in a row played (tracked with Devvit Journeys) |

### Fading and return

When your civilization's People drop below 1k, it collapses. Its cities become ruins and you **fade**:

- Trust income drops to 5 a day. You keep the Trust you already have.
- You draw 1 card a day, and it can only be Whisper or Sign.
- You can still dig, make prophecies and write scripture.

**Return:** once your Remembrance reaches 5, or the moment you make a Homecoming (section 7), you can Whisper to a wild people or an untended ruin region. If the Whisper succeeds, that region becomes your new home with 3k People, and the Chronicle announces it: *"The forgotten Ascendant Vaelor has returned."*

## 4. World and civilizations

The world is one hand-shaped hex map of **88 regions**: 64 known regions plus the 24-region Dark Continent, which starts in fog. Every civilization is described by four stats, so the whole state fits on one phone screen.

### Regions

| Field | Values |
| --- | --- |
| Terrain | Plains, Forest, Hills, Desert, Coast, Water, Swamp, Tundra, Mountain (only Stonebound, Titanborn and Golemkind can settle it) |
| Owner | An Ascendant (player or Old One), Wild, Ruin or Wasteland |
| Corruption | 0–100 (section 10) |
| Ruin depth | 0–5 layers of buried history (section 7) |

When a later season needs more space, add a second map instead of making this one bigger.

### The four civilization stats

| Stat | Range | What drives it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| People | in thousands | Grows when Food has a surplus, falls in famine, war and Corruption |
| Food | 0–100 stock | Terrain plus blessings, minus what People eat |
| Faith | Devoted / Faithful / Doubtful split | Divine actions, prosperity, rival conversion, Corruption |
| Might | 0–100 | Teach cards and champions. Decides expansion and defence |

### How civilizations behave (rule-based, every 5-year tick)

- **Grow** when Food is above 60: People +2%.
- **Starve** when Food is below 20: People −4%, and Doubtful followers rise.
- **Expand** into a neighbouring Wild or Ruin region when People is above 8k per region owned and Might is above 30.
- **Raid** a weaker neighbour (their Might is at least 20 below ours) with a 10% chance. Raids produce war ruins.
- **Lose faith:** if Doubtful followers go above 50%, a faction splits off and the region goes Wild.

All randomness comes from a seeded generator (seed = world id + leap number + a secret server salt, so leaps can't be predicted), so a replayed leap always gives the same result.

### Wild peoples

About a third of regions start as independent peoples with no Ascendant. Any Ascendant can Whisper to them to begin conversion: contact, then acceptance, then the region joins once more than 50% of its people believe. Several Ascendants can compete for the same wild region. Each region's Tradition (0–3) makes conversion slower.

### Species and rarity

There are **21 species in 5 rarity tiers**. Players can draw 18 of them; the 3 Legendary species belong only to the Old Ones. **Rarer doesn't mean stronger.** A rarer species gets a sharper speciality but starts in a harder place and grows more slowly, and every Epic species has a Common species that counters it. Species aren't unique: the same species can live in many parts of the world, under many Ascendants, and in wild regions.

