### Why rare doesn't win by default

- **Numbers matter.** Raid and defence strength = Might × √People (in thousands) × terrain × counter bonus. A large Common empire beats a small Epic one.
- **Harder starts.** Epic and Rare species start with the same 3k People, but in poorer land and closer to danger (see the rarity table).
- **Counters.** Every Epic has a Common counter that gets ×1.5 strength against it.

### Why lifespan matters

- **Generations:** a champion lives as long as their species. A 100-year leap moves a Gnarling bloodline on 4 generations, while a Sylvan hero lives through it.
- **Memory:** each tick, belief drifts toward Doubtful by 1% × (60 / lifespan), and never by more than 8 points in one leap, Fickle included. Short-lived peoples forget their Ascendant quickly but also take up new faiths quickly.
- **Pacing:** short-lived species rise and fall within days. Long-lived species last for weeks and leave the deepest ruins.

### The Dark Continent

A separate landmass of **24 regions** sits in fog from day one, so the full map is 88 regions. It holds the lairs of the Legendary Old Ones, wild Rare and Epic peoples, and ruins that start 3–5 layers deep. The Corruption is twice as likely to begin here.

- **Exploring:** the Explore card reveals one fogged region next to known land. A champion on Expedition reveals 2 a leap, and Duskwings reveal one extra.
- **Risk:** 20% of expeditions are lost, and a revealed Wyrm lair may wake early.
- **Naming rights:** the first Ascendant to reveal a region names it, up to 24 characters and profanity-filtered. That name stays on the map for everyone.

## 5. Trust economy and action cards

Trust is earned from your followers, so a larger and more devoted people gives more power. Every cost and reward also grows with your civilization, so Trust stays meaningful at any size and a big empire can't simply outspend everyone. All numbers in this section are starting values for playtesting.

### Trust income (paid once per daily leap)

```latex
\text{Trust}_{day} = 3 + 1.5 \times \text{People}_{k} \times (3\,d + f)
```

Here d and f are the shares of Devoted and Faithful followers. Doubtful followers add nothing, and the flat 3 is only a floor. A new people of 3k with 30% Devoted and 50% Faithful earns 3 + 1.5 × 3 × 1.4 = **9.3 Trust** a day; at 30k People it earns **66**. A fading Ascendant earns a flat 3. Trust has no cap. Each region beyond your first 4 costs 1 base Trust a day in upkeep.

Other Trust sources: a correct prophecy (11–80 Trust, by the odds), finding an artifact (+5), one of your own artifacts being found (+5 Trust and +1 Remembrance), and damaging the Evil One (Slayer bonus).

### Costs and rewards grow with you

Every cost and reward in this spec is a **base** amount. The real amount is the base multiplied by your **Weight**:

```latex
\text{Weight} = \max\left(1,\ \sqrt{\frac{\text{People}_{k}}{3}}\right) \times \min\left(2,\ 1 + 0.02 \times \text{generations}\right)
```

- **Size:** the square root of your People (in thousands) divided by 3, never below 1. A 3k people has size 1, 12k has size 2, and 30k about 3.2.
- **Age:** +2% for every generation (25 years) your civilization has lived, up to ×2. As time passes, everything you do costs more.

Income grows in a straight line with followers, while costs grow only with the square root of size. So a bigger, more devoted people is stronger, but not overwhelmingly so. For example, 30k People after 20 generations have a Weight of about 4.4. They earn 66 Trust a day, which buys about 15 base Trust of actions, against 9.3 for a newcomer.

**What scales with Weight:**

- **Costs:** cards (table below), commissioning artifacts, champion and Saint orders, Envoy messages, Seek the Lost and upkeep.
- **Rewards:** dig finds, Shrines, Homecoming, prophecy payouts, the Seeker reward and Destroy. A find feels worth the same to every player.
- **Pledges against the Evil One count in base Trust** (Trust pledged ÷ Weight), so a small people's pledge hits as hard as a large one's.

### Action cards

Each day you draw 3 cards (weighted by your domain) and play up to 2. Cards you don't play are discarded. Every card **starts a process** that resolves over the next leap or leaps.

| Card | Base cost (× Weight) | Effect | Shows up |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Bless Harvest | 6 | Food +25 over the next 3 ticks | Next leap |
| Reveal Spring | 5 | Region's Food cap +15 permanently | Next leap |
| Inspire Leader | 10 | +15% chance to expand, Doubtful −5% | 1–2 leaps |
| Teach | 10 | Might +20 (or Food yield +10% with Harvest), builds up over 25 years | Long leaps pay more |
| Whisper | 4 | Begin or advance conversion of a wild region; usable while fading | 1–3 leaps |
| Sign | 3 | Doubtful −3%; a fading Ascendant can use it to make one of their ruins glow on every map, so diggers find it | Next leap |
| Smite | 15 | Target region: People −10%, Might −15; leaves war ruins. A second Smite on the same region in one leap does half | Next leap |
| Ward | 8 | Blocks Corruption spreading into one region this leap | Next leap |
| Cleanse | 12 | Corruption −30 in one region | Next leap |
| Explore | 8 | Reveal one fogged Dark Continent region next to known land; 20% chance the expedition is lost | Next leap |
| Commission | 15 | Your people build a named artifact of your species' kind (temple, idol, weapon or scripture), carved with your scripture line; Devoted +3%. It becomes a diggable relic if the region falls | Next leap |
| Envoy | 2 | Send a message of up to 140 characters to another Ascendant. It arrives at the next leap and is written into both Books | Next leap |
| Raise Chosen | 50 | Founds your bloodline (section 8); one time only | Immediately |

**Spending Trust also builds belief:** every card you play on your own people turns 1% of Doubtful followers into Faithful. Smiting a region near your own lowers your followers' faith by 2%, because fear works differently from devotion.

## 6. Time leaps

Each day the world leaps forward by **5 to 300 years**, drawn from a fixed distribution. Most leaps are short: a leap of 100 years or more comes about once a week, and a Great Age about once every 3 weeks. The simulation runs one tick per 5 years, up to 60 ticks.

| Leap | Chance | Ticks | What it typically does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 5 years | 30% | 1 | Card effects land; small shifts in Food and Faith |
| 25 years | 35% | 5 | One generation passes; Teach pays off; expansion and raids |
| 50 years | 20% | 10 | Borders move; champions may die of old age; Corruption spreads 2–3 regions |
| 100 years | 10% | 20 | Empires rise or fall; ruins form; bloodlines move on 2–4 generations |
| 300 years | 5% | 60 | A new age: collapses, returns, ruins buried one layer deeper, Corruption surges |

### Rules

- **The leap size is announced first.** The Chronicle opens with it: "**A Great Age passes: 300 years.**"
- **No more than 2 big leaps (100+ years) in any 7 days**, so the world never moves too fast to follow.
- **During the Corruption's Spread phase leaps are capped at 50 years, and while the Evil One is out they are capped at 25 years**, so the final fight lasts several real days.
- **Leap names** add flavour: 5 = *A Season*, 25 = *A Generation*, 50 = *An Era*, 100 = *An Age*, 300 = *A Great Age*.
- **The Prophecy step** asks players to guess tomorrow's leap name. Payouts follow the odds (A Season 13 Trust, A Generation 11, An Era 20, An Age 40, A Great Age 80), so no single guess is always best.

## 7. Ruins, digging and artifacts

Ruins are where the world's history piles up, and digging is how players reach each other across time. When a player finds another Ascendant's artifact, both Ascendants gain from it.

### How ruins form

- A region becomes a Ruin when its civilization collapses, an Old One fades, a raid or Smite levels it, or the Corruption consumes it.
- Every collapse adds **one ruin layer** (up to 5). Each layer holds the artifacts of the civilization that fell there.
- A Great Age (300-year) leap buries every ruin one layer deeper. Deeper layers are rarer and worth more.

### Digging (3 digs a day, on any visible ruin)

| Result | Chance | Reward |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Dust | 45% | Nothing, but a short line of lore |
| Fragment | 35% | +2 Trust |
| Artifact | 17% | +5 Trust; added to your Reliquary; the creator Ascendant gains +5 Trust and +1 Remembrance |
| Great Relic | 3% (Death domain 5%) | +15 Trust, a one-time power (below), and a Chronicle headline |

When an artifact belongs to a player, they get a notification: *"Your Drowned Bell was unearthed by u/someone, 400 years after your fall."*

### Artifacts

| Field | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Kind | Temple, Idol, Weapon, Scripture, Relic |
| Name | The Drowned Bell |
| Creator Ascendant and civilization | Vaelor, the Tide-Folk of Ostra |
| Created in year, condition | Year 412, Weathered |
| Inscription | The creator Ascendant's scripture line, carved on it |

Artifacts are created automatically: a temple after 50 years of strong faith, a weapon when a hero dies, scripture when a saint dies. **Great Relics** have a one-time power. *Shard of the Evil One* lowers a region's Corruption by 50. *Crown of a Fallen Empire* adds +3 Remembrance to the Ascendant who made it.

### Ruin realms

A ruin takes the shape of the species that built it, so ruins appear across five realms. Each realm changes who can reach it, and the map shows each one differently.

| Realm | Built by | Who can dig it | On the map |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Surface | Humans, Hoofkin, Emberkin, Fangkin, Gnarlings, Sylvan, Mycelids | Anyone who owns a neighbouring region | Cracked stone tiles |
| Deep (underground) | Burrowkin, Stonebound, Golemkind, Shadeborn | Anyone, but each dig costs 2 digs unless you are a delving species (Burrowkin, Stonebound, Golemkind) | Sealed doors in the earth |
| Peak (mountain) | Titanborn, Frostkin, Stonebound mountain halls | Owners of a neighbouring Hills or Mountain region, or a champion on Expedition | Broken spires on summits |
| Sunken (sea) | Tidefolk, Naga, Mirefolk | Owners of a neighbouring Coast region, sea species (Tidefolk, Naga, Mirefolk) or the Sea domain | Drowned towers under the waves |
| Sky | Duskwings, Ashborne, Seraphs | Hidden until revealed by Explore; flying species (Duskwings, Ashborne) see them at once | A faint shimmer in the clouds |

### Discovering a ruin: Claim, Destroy or Leave

When you dig an Artifact or Great Relic from a ruin, you have **discovered** it. You then make one choice for that ruin:

| Choice | You get | The Ascendant who built it gets | Other effects |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Claim** | It becomes your Shrine: +2 Trust a day while you hold it (up to 3 Shrines) | +1 Remembrance a day while it stands, because their memory is kept alive | Another Ascendant can take your Shrine by conquering the region it stands in |
| **Destroy** | +20 Trust once | That layer's artifacts turn to dust, but their followers' anger gives them +10 Trust once | Your Doubtful followers +3%; the Chronicle calls you *the Defiler* for 7 days; the Seraph Old One may punish you |
| **Leave** | You keep the artifact you dug | +1 Remembrance (the usual reward) | The ruin stays open for others to find |

### Homecoming

Finding a ruin that **you built** is a Homecoming. That means your own fallen civilization, or the ruin your Cradle left behind. It pays **+25 Trust and +3 Remembrance**. If you are fading, you can **Return on the spot**, reclaiming that region with 3k People. The Chronicle marks it: *"After 600 years, Vaelor walked again among the halls of Ostra."*

## 8. Bloodlines: heroes and saints

Each Ascendant can found **one Chosen bloodline** for 50 base Trust, but only when at least 25% of their followers are Devoted. Champions are mortal, so the time leaps turn every bloodline into a family saga.

### Two lines

| Line | Strong at | Leaves behind at death |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hero | Fighting Corruption, defending, conquest (Might +15 while alive) | A weapon artifact, e.g. *The Spear of Kael* |
| Saint | Cleansing, conversion, faith (Doubtful −2% each leap) | A holy relic plus a scripture artifact |

### Generations

- **A champion lives for 40–70 years** of world time. A 100-year leap means the line moves on 2–4 generations in a single day.
- **Each heir gets a generated name and number:** *Kael III, the Oathkeeper*.
- **Heirs inherit one of the parent's traits.** There's a 30% chance of gaining a new trait, and a champion can hold at most 3.
- **A line ends** if your civilization falls while the current champion has no heir. You can found a new line for 50 base Trust.

### Traits (6 in v1)

| Trait | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| Fireborn | +50% damage to the Evil One |
| Oathkeeper | Your home region can't be raided |
| Seer | Your prophecy pays double |
| Ironblood | Lives 20 years longer |
| Silver-Tongued | Whisper conversion is 2× faster |
| Cursed | Picked up after spending 50+ years in a region with Corruption above 50. Each leap, a 15% chance to turn into a **Herald of the Evil One** |

When a Cursed champion turns, they leave your bloodline and fight for the Corruption. A Saint's Cleanse can remove the Cursed trait before that happens.

### Champion orders (one a day)

- **Quest:** go to a corrupted region and fight, lowering Corruption or damaging the Evil One.
- **Pilgrimage:** speed up conversion of a wild region.
- **Defend:** guard your home, adding +20 Might against raids and Corruption. A fourth order, Expedition, sends the champion to reveal 2 Dark Continent regions a leap, with the same 20% risk of being lost.

**Order costs grow with the line.** Orders cost base Trust × Weight: Quest 8, Pilgrimage 6, Defend 4, Expedition 8. A champion's power grows by 10% for every generation of their bloodline, and so does the cost of commanding them. An ancient line of Saints cleanses more Corruption and converts faster, but every order to them costs more.

**Titles:** the champion who lands the final blow on the Evil One becomes *Slayer of \<name>*. The title shows on your share card and stays with the bloodline forever.

## 9. Old Ones (bot Ascendants)

Old Ones play by exactly the same rules and Trust budget as players. They keep the world lively when few players are around, and they step aside as more players join. They're rule-based and seeded (no AI model), and every bot is clearly marked with a sigil and an "Old One" label.

### Personalities

| Type | Domain | Behaviour | Typical fate |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| The Devourer | War | Expands fast and attacks the weakest neighbour | Falls early, leaving many ruins |
| The Shepherd | Harvest | Grows slowly, blesses neighbours, defends small Ascendants | Long-lived ally |
| The Hermit | Wisdom | Stays small and isolated, builds temples | Leaves the best artifacts |
| The Trickster | Sea | Steals followers from whoever leads in Faith | Keeps the leaders in check |
| The Pale One | Death | Grows when others collapse, digs relics, may side with the Corruption | Wildcard |

Your team writes **12 Old Ones** by hand, each with a name, epithet, species, sigil and a scripture line, so they feel authored rather than generated.

**Legendary Old Ones.** Besides the 12 ordinary Old Ones, each world has 3 Legendary Old Ones in the Dark Continent: a Seraph host, an Infernal court and a Wyrm (section 4). They never fade and can't be converted. They act on their own rules: the Seraph punishes whoever Smites most, the Infernals offer pacts, and the Wyrm wakes on Great Age leaps. They give players something larger than themselves to fear, bargain with or dig beneath. They don't count toward the 12-Ascendant minimum.

### Decisions

Each bot acts during step 2 of the leap (section 13). It draws a hand, then scores every card with its personality's weight table plus a few rules, for example "if Food < 30, play Bless Harvest" or "Smite whoever Smote me last."

```ts
for (const bot of bots) {
  const hand = drawHand(bot, seed);
  const pick = maxBy(hand, c => PERSONALITY[bot.type].score(c, world, bot.memory));
  if (pick && bot.faith >= pick.cost) queueAction(bot, pick);
}
```

**Memory:** each bot keeps a grudge or favour score (−10 to +10) for every Ascendant that has affected it. Grudges fade by 1 each leap. This is what produces Chronicle lines like *"Morrak remembers the drought Vaelor sent."*

### Keeping the right number of Ascendants

- **Target:** one world per subreddit, with no cap on players, so everyone shares one Chronicle and one Evil One. Old Ones keep at least 12 Ascendants active and fill empty land, not player seats. For every 12 new Ascendants, a new ring of 12 regions, including reserved landing regions, rises from the sea at the edge of the known map.
- **Bots step aside:** when a player joins near a bot, that bot gets a 3-leap "Twilight." Its People fall by 30% each leap, then its regions become ruins full of its artifacts. Bots never simply disappear.
- **Bots can return:** Remembrance works the same for bots, so a player digging up their relics can bring an Old One back.

### Fairness

- Bots are shown greyed out on the leaderboards and can never be ranked #1.
- Bots never earn or spend more Trust than the formula allows.
- No one, player or Old One, can Smite or raid an Ascendant in their first 3 days after leaving the Cradle.

## 10. The Corruption (seasonal event)

Each season lasts **8–10 real days** and follows four phases. Corruption takes root in a hidden place and spreads further with every leap, and nobody is told where until a player finds it. An Evil One emerges, and the whole subreddit wins or loses together. Then the next season starts somewhere else with a different Evil One.

1. **Omens (days 1–2).** The Corruption takes root in a **secret place** chosen by the server. It might be a fogged Dark Continent region, a Deep, Sunken or Sky ruin, or under an ordinary region near the ruins of the *most forgotten Old One*. No one is told where. The Chronicle carries only vague omens (*"Somewhere, the rivers run black"*).
2. **Spread (days 3–6).** Each tick, every region with Corruption above 40 has a 12% chance to spread +20 to each neighbour (at most 3 new regions per leap), so **bigger leaps spread it further**. Above 60 Corruption, a region loses 3% People and 3% Faith per tick. At 100 it falls, becomes Hollowed, and its people worship the Evil One. Until the source is found, owners of affected regions see only *"a sickness with no name"*: their losses, but not the cause or where it came from.
3. **Emergence (days 7–8+).** Once 8 regions are Hollowed, or on day 7 at the latest, the Evil One wakes in a **hidden lair**. The Chronicle says only *"Something has awoken."* Its name, form, health and lair stay secret until someone finds it. Its attacks land on regions near the lair, and each attack is a clue. Leaps are capped at 25 years while it's awake.
4. **Aftermath.** If players **win**, it shatters into Great Relics (*Shard of Vashtar*) that the top contributors receive. If players **lose**, after 3 days the Hollowed regions become a permanent Wasteland: deep ruins with dangerous but rare finds.

### Hidden until discovered

The Corruption and the Evil One are a **mystery to solve**. The world can feel their effects, but nobody learns where they are or what they are until a player finds them.

**What stays secret:** where the Corruption started, how corrupted any region is (except your own symptoms), and the Evil One's name, form, health and lair.

**The Omens panel:** every daily Chronicle post carries the day's omens, and they sharpen as the season goes on:

| Season day | What the omen reveals | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1–2 | Only a mood | *"Somewhere, the rivers run black."* |
| 3 | The realm or terrain | *"It sleeps beneath deep water."* |
| 4 | A direction from the world's centre | *"In the far east, the tides have turned."* |
| 5+ | A nearby landmark or named region | *"Within sight of the Spires of Kael."* |

**Private clues:** an Ascendant whose region is touched by the sickness gets one extra clue that only they can see. Champions with the Seer trait and Ascendants of the Death domain receive each clue one day early. Players can share or hide what they know, so the comment thread becomes the hunt.

**How it's discovered:** dig the ruin it hides in, Explore its fogged region, send a champion on Expedition or Quest into it, or Whisper to a Hollowed people, who reveal their master. A wrong guess costs only that action.

**On discovery, everything is revealed to everyone:**

- The Chronicle headline names the discoverer: *"u/someone found the heart of the Blight in the drowned towers of Ostra."*
- The discoverer gets **+50 Trust** and the permanent title *Seeker of the Blight*, written into their Book.
- The map shows every corrupted region, and Ward and Cleanse can target anywhere. Before discovery, players can only Ward and Cleanse regions of their own that show symptoms.
- Finding the Evil One's lair is a **second discovery**. It reveals the Evil One's name and form, puts its health bar in the daily post and opens one-tap pledging.

**Safety net:** if no one has found the lair by day 9, the Evil One reveals itself (*"It shows itself at last"*), with no Seeker reward, so every season still gets its final battle.

**Kept on the server:** the hidden location and levels are never sent to players' devices. The game only receives omen text and facts that have been discovered, so nobody can find the secret by inspecting the game's data.

### The Evil One

| Property | v1 value |
| --- | --- |
| Health | 150 + 20 × active Ascendants (390 with 12, about 4 days of shared pledging) |
| Damage sources | Trust pledged at its lair, counted in base Trust (Trust ÷ Weight), a Hero on Quest 40 (Fireborn ×1.5), Cleanse on its lair 25 |
| Its attack each leap | Corruption +20 in 2 regions bordering the Hollowed area |
| Grows from | +3 health per Hollowed region each leap |
| Pool | 4 hand-written Evil Ones (e.g. *Vashtar, the Hunger Beneath*), rotated by season |

### Counterplay

Players fight it with **Ward** (stop spread), **Cleanse** (reduce Corruption), Hero quests, Saint cleansing, and spending Trust at the lair. Bots join in according to their personality: the Shepherd defends, the Devourer ignores it, and the Pale One may *feed* it.

**The community bar:** once its lair has been found, the daily Chronicle post shows the Evil One's health bar and the top 5 contributors. Pledging Trust is one tap from the post, which gives the whole subreddit a shared goal.

**For judging:** time the first season so that the hunt is on when judges arrive, with the Corruption spreading and the Omens panel filling up. Consider letting the source be discovered on Dec 7, so judges see both the mystery and the revealed map. The pinned post should open with a 3-line "story so far" recap.

## 11. Reddit integration

The game happens in the subreddit as much as in the post. The daily Chronicle post is the front page of the world, and its comments are the world's diplomacy.

| Feature | How it works |
| --- | --- |
| Daily Chronicle post | After each leap the app automatically posts a new interactive post, titled e.g. *"Year 1,240: The Drought of Ash (An Era, 50 years)"*. Its preview shows the leap, 3 headlines and the Evil One's bar |
| Comments as diplomacy | Alliances, threats and boasting happen in the thread. The game doesn't need a diplomacy system |
| Scripture | Each Ascendant writes one line, up to 80 characters, filtered for profanity. Players upvote lines inside the game. Each day the top line is quoted in the Chronicle and carved onto that Ascendant's next artifact |
| Prophecy | One guess a day (the leap name, or whether a named Ascendant survives). It resolves in the next Chronicle, pays by the odds (section 6), and keeps a prophecy streak |
| Share card | The end-of-session image shows the Ascendant's sigil, year, one-line myth and Slayer title if any. **Post as comment** puts it in the daily thread |
| Leaderboards | Most Remembered, Longest Bloodline, Oldest Surviving Relic, Most Devoted, Slayers. Bots appear greyed out |
| Streaks | Daily play streak and prophecy streak, tracked with Devvit Journeys; a flame next to the Ascendant's name |
| Return notifications | "Your relic was found" and "Your champion died" pull lapsed players back (if Devvit allows it, otherwise shown in the next session) |

### The Library: Books and the Annals

The world writes down its own history, and **anyone can read it**: players, lurkers and judges, even people who have never joined.

- **A Book for every civilization** (*The Book of Ostra, a Tidefolk people*). The leap engine writes every event into it automatically: the founding, expansions, raids and wars, famines, conversions, champions born and dying, scripture lines, artifacts made, found, claimed or destroyed, Homecomings, and the fall. A new chapter begins with every Great Age.
- **The Faithful:** each Book ends with a roll of honour for its people of great faith. Saints, Heroes, Slayers of the Evil One and the most devoted pledgers are named there, with how long they lived and what they did.
- **The Annals of the World:** the history of the whole world, with one entry for every leap (each day's Chronicle bound in order). It also covers every Corruption season, Legendary Old One event and Dark Continent discovery, along with the names their discoverers gave them.
- **Shelves by species:** the Library is arranged by species. Opening the Tidefolk shelf shows every Tidefolk civilization that ever existed, living and fallen, side by side.
- **Sealed when a people falls:** when a civilization collapses, its Book gets a final chapter (*The Fall of Ostra*) and is sealed. It stays readable, and a copy is buried in its ruin as a Great Relic. Whoever digs it up is written into the Book as *Rediscovered by*, and for the Ascendant who built it, that counts as a Homecoming.
- **Shareable:** every page has its own link, and each daily Chronicle post ends with *Read the full Annals*.

**How it's built:** the leap engine already writes Chronicle lines in step 8 (section 13). Every line is also added to an append-only history log for its civilization and for the world (`book:{civ}` and `annals:{world}` in Redis). The Library screen only reads those logs, page by page, so the feature costs little to build.

