## 12. UI and visual identity

The whole game lives on **one map screen** with sheets that slide over it, designed mobile-first at 375 px wide. The art style is **an illuminated manuscript map**: ink linework on parchment, gold leaf for anything divine, and the Corruption in creeping black-violet ink.

### Screen layout

| Area | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| Top bar | Year, today's leap badge (*An Era*), Trust, streak flame |
| Map (centre) | Pan and zoom hex map, your territory outlined in gold, ruins as cracked tiles, Corruption as spreading ink |
| Card tray (bottom) | 3 cards dealt face-up; drag a card onto a region to play it |
| Chronicle sheet | Slides up first each day: the leap, 3 headlines, your own story |
| Ascendant sheet | Tap your sigil: stats, bloodline, Reliquary, scripture |
| End card | The share card plus "Return tomorrow" countdown |
| Library | Shelves by species; open any civilization's Book or the Annals of the World; every page shareable |

### Making it feel good

- **Animate the leap:** a short ink-wash sweeps across the map, the year ticks up, and fallen cities crack into ruins.
- **Make digging tactile:** tap, brush away dust, then reveal what's underneath (Dust, Fragment, Artifact or the gold glow of a Great Relic).
- **Show history on the map:** older ruin layers look more faded, and Wastelands stay as dark scars.
- **Every card shows** its cost, a one-line effect and *when* it lands ("next leap", "over a generation").

### Style rules (to avoid looking AI-generated)

- Hand-draw or commission a small set of assets: 7 terrain tiles, 10 card illustrations, 5 domain sigils, 21 species emblems (10 needed for P0), ruin tiles, plus the Evil One. Don't use AI-generated art.
- Use one display typeface (a calligraphic serif) plus one body face. Use the palette ink, parchment, gold, and corruption violet, and nothing else.
- No Reddit orange and no Snoo; the game should be recognisable as itself.

### What players see, by day

The game reveals itself gradually, so the first day is simple and the depth arrives over the first week. Nothing is cut: each part just appears when the player is ready for it.

| Day | What becomes visible |
| --- | --- |
| Day 1 (Cradle and first shared day) | Trust, People, Food; cards Bless, Whisper, Ward, Explore; digging; prophecy; the Chronicle and the Library |
| Day 2 | The belief split (Devoted, Faithful, Doubtful); Smite, Sign and Envoy; Claim, Destroy or Leave |
| Days 3–4 | Might, raids, Teach, Inspire and Commission; Seek the Lost; leaderboards |
| Day 5+ | Raise Chosen and bloodlines; ruin realms beyond Surface; Expeditions into the Dark Continent |
| During a Corruption season | The Omens panel only. Full Corruption and Evil One details appear only after a player discovers them (section 10) |

## 13. Technical architecture

Only one daily server job changes the world. Player sessions only read state and queue actions, so thousands of Ascendants can share one world without conflicting writes.

&#91;embedded content: Faithfall architecture · play path and daily leap\]

The top row runs every time someone opens the post. The bottom row runs once a day and is the only thing that advances history.

### Stack

| Layer | Choice |
| --- | --- |
| Platform | Devvit Web, as an Interactive Post |
| Client | React + TypeScript; hex map on HTML canvas (PixiJS only if it's needed for performance) |
| Server | Devvit Web server endpoints, TypeScript |
| Storage | Devvit Redis |
| Daily leap | Devvit scheduler, cron job at 14:00 UTC |
| Streaks and analytics | Devvit Journeys |
| Shared code | One `sim/` package used by the server *and* the offline history seeder |

### Core data models

```ts
type Domain = 'war' | 'harvest' | 'death' | 'sea' | 'wisdom';

interface World   { id: string; kind: 'shared' | 'cradle'; ownerId?: string; leap: number; year: number; seed: string; seasonId?: string; }
interface Ascendant { id: string; userId?: string; isBot: boolean; botType?: BotType;
                    name: string; epithet: string; domain: Domain; trust: number;
                    remembrance: number; status: 'active' | 'fading' | 'returned';
                    scripture?: string; streak: number; memory?: Record<string, number>; }
interface Region  { id: number; terrain: Terrain; owner: string | 'wild' | 'ruin' | 'waste';
                    species: SpeciesId; people: number; food: number; might: number;
                    belief: { devoted: number; faithful: number; doubtful: number };
                    corruption: number; ruinRealm?: 'surface' | 'deep' | 'peak' | 'sunken' | 'sky'; ruinLayers: string[][]; }   // artifact ids per layer
interface Artifact{ id: string; kind: ArtifactKind; name: string; ascendantId: string;
                    yearMade: number; layer: number; foundBy?: string; claimedBy?: string; inscription?: string; }
interface Champion{ id: string; ascendantId: string; line: 'hero' | 'saint'; name: string;
                    generation: number; born: number; traits: Trait[]; order?: Order; }
interface Action  { ascendantId: string; card: CardId; target?: number; leap: number; }
interface Season  { id: string; phase: 'omens' | 'spread' | 'emergence' | 'aftermath';
                    evilOneId: string; hp: number; contributors: Record<string, number>; }
```

Redis keys: `world:{id}` (hash), `ascendant:{id}`, `region:{world}:{n}`, `actions:{world}:{leap}` (list), `lock:leap:{world}:{leap}`, `chronicle:{world}:{leap}`.

### Leap engine, in order

1. **Lock:** `SET lock:leap:{world}:{n} NX`. If the lock exists or `world.leap` is already n, stop. This makes retries safe.
2. **Roll the leap size** from the seed (world id + n), applying the caps in section 6.
3. **Let bots choose actions** and add them to the queue.
4. **Apply all queued actions**, which start processes.
5. **For each 5-year tick:** resolve processes, then Food, then People, then the belief split, then expansion and raids, Corruption spread, champion ageing and heirs, and artifact creation.
6. **Handle collapses and returns:** fallen cities become ruins, Ascendants fade or return.
7. **Pay Trust income;** apply damage to the Evil One and its growth.
8. **Write the Chronicle** from hand-written templates (no AI model), with 3 world headlines plus a personal line for each Ascendant.
9. **Commit everything in one transaction,** including `world.leap = n`.
10. **Publish the Chronicle post.**

**Seeded history:** before launch, run the same engine offline for about 45 leaps (around 2,000 years) with bots only. Load the result as the starting world, so judges arrive to find existing ruins, fallen empires and buried relics.

**Cradle worlds:** each Cradle is a tiny World with `kind: 'cradle'`, saved per player. Its leaps run on demand through the same engine, with no scheduler and no shared lock. It is deleted after Ascension, apart from the ruin it leaves in the shared world.

